WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:04.250
Lastly, today we'll talk about subsurface materials

2
00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:05.929
and parallax control.

3
00:00:05.929 --> 00:00:08.810
So here we'll talk about creating a subsurface material,

4
00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.029
which we'll use Jade in this instance.

5
00:00:11.029 --> 00:00:13.970
We'll talk about using parallax occlusion mapping

6
00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:14.970
with material functions.

7
00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.750
We'll talk about creating some ice

8
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.730
and how you may go about doing that.

9
00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.809
I will talk about the subsurface profile in Unreal

10
00:00:21.809 --> 00:00:24.949
and then also give you some pro tips to close out here.

11
00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:26.449
So there's a few things to consider

12
00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:29.410
if we're thinking about creating subsurface materials

13
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.969
and this instance, like we said,

14
00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:34.399
we're going to use the example of Jade-like material.

15
00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:39.000
So creating something like Jade or even ice,

16
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.399
you can make some dependencies

17
00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:42.820
to make the material rich with control.

18
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.200
So you have plenty of control for your artists

19
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.399
to really dial in the effects that they're wanting to use.

20
00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:51.560
So we set up the material to be a subsurface

21
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.200
under the shaded model.

22
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.420
So when you have your material open,

23
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.920
you want to make sure you're not clicking on any nodes.

24
00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.399
You can click on the main root node

25
00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:01.399
that you can see in the bottom of the image here,

26
00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:04.719
but essentially you'll see this on the details panel

27
00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:05.560
in your material.

28
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.700
So you just want to change the shading model

29
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.280
to subsurface to texturize the effect.

30
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.670
Color can be stacked with lerps and multiplies

31
00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:16.989
to get the desired results.

32
00:01:16.989 --> 00:01:18.909
So that's exactly what you can see here.

33
00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:20.989
We've got two color values.

34
00:01:20.989 --> 00:01:24.390
We're lerping, which is the linear interpolate

35
00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.469
between this noise-based texture,

36
00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:31.170
which then multiplying based on the amount that you want.

37
00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:35.469
And you're making parameters of all these properties

38
00:01:35.469 --> 00:01:37.250
so that you can dial them in

39
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:38.909
either in your material parameter collection,

40
00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:41.650
like you said before, that you saw before,

41
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.189
or whether you just want it

42
00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:46.359
on a per material instance basis.

43
00:01:46.359 --> 00:01:49.260
So the final texture output can have a scaler on it

44
00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.560
to control the amount of noise filter to use.

45
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.280
So this is plugged into the lerp.

46
00:01:54.319 --> 00:01:57.840
So you can see the amount of noise here,

47
00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.739
which is the noise being multiplied.

48
00:02:00.739 --> 00:02:03.560
And this is the point that we're talking about here

49
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.450
just before the end result.

50
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.650
If you want to control the opacity

51
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:09.810
of a subsurface material,

52
00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.810
you can create a Fresnel index

53
00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.310
and control the exponent there.

54
00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.370
So you introduce a lerp and a scaler

55
00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.750
to control the subsurface amount,

56
00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.689
which is the opacity that is used

57
00:02:22.689 --> 00:02:24.189
for the transmission here.

58
00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.870
And then also you can add a multiply into this lerp B

59
00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.550
to then multiply the subsurface amount effect.

60
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.349
So it can be adjusted with a new input,

61
00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:36.250
but it can be used as a generic range control

62
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.300
in this instance.

63
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.060
To add subsurface control,

64
00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.379
we create a Fresnel node as well, again,

65
00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:46.060
and then this helps sell the idea

66
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.580
of some kind of light affecting the surface.

67
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.969
It gives it that rim light and look.

68
00:02:50.969 --> 00:02:53.330
You could create a scaler for the exponent.

69
00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:57.810
And in this case, it controls the edge subsurface control.

70
00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.090
And then you can also create a scaler

71
00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.129
for the specular reflection.

72
00:03:02.129 --> 00:03:04.689
In this case, it'll give the illusion of thickness.

73
00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:09.150
So plug that into the base reflection fraction in,

74
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.210
and then you can also plug in a multiplier

75
00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:15.639
with a cons three vector to control the overall color.

76
00:03:15.639 --> 00:03:17.479
So a few notes here

77
00:03:17.479 --> 00:03:20.560
that when we're adding subsurface control,

78
00:03:20.759 --> 00:03:23.680
the Fresnel that is found under the vector ops

79
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.379
is a highly customized version of the Fresnel.

80
00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:29.919
So that allows you to affect many different aspects

81
00:03:29.919 --> 00:03:31.219
of how the Fresnel is rendered.

82
00:03:31.219 --> 00:03:33.560
So you can see the properties here.

83
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.199
There's great documentation on this.

84
00:03:35.199 --> 00:03:37.080
This image is taken from the documentation.

85
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.879
So they allow you to dial in the Fresnel values

86
00:03:39.879 --> 00:03:41.479
that you're looking for.

87
00:03:41.479 --> 00:03:43.699
This is the Fresnel node,

88
00:03:43.699 --> 00:03:46.159
but just to know that it can give more control,

89
00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:49.099
but it can lead to artifacts if not set up correctly.

90
00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:54.060
So only use Fresnel, the regular Fresnel,

91
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:58.379
if this version isn't getting the results that you want,

92
00:03:58.379 --> 00:04:00.159
and also if you don't understand

93
00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:03.139
some of the properties found there.

94
00:04:03.139 --> 00:04:05.939
If we're looking at parallax occlusion mapping,

95
00:04:05.939 --> 00:04:09.840
so the parallax occlusion and material function node,

96
00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.699
we use it to simulate a 3D effect.

97
00:04:12.699 --> 00:04:15.819
So it's like a bump offset, but with more visual accuracy.

98
00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:19.060
So no real distance limitations.

99
00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.259
It is expensive.

100
00:04:21.259 --> 00:04:23.220
The visualize will not calculate correctly,

101
00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.220
so it will stay green appearing to be safe.

102
00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.000
So when you're looking at your view render modes

103
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.139
and you're looking at how expensive this might be,

104
00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:34.540
it appears safe, but it does have a lot of complexity

105
00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:38.139
to it, as you can see with the complexity of the material.

106
00:04:38.139 --> 00:04:43.180
So material collection is the shadows

107
00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.319
and the light source reaction are more realistic

108
00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:48.000
at an angle than a bump offset.

109
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.360
So it can save on needed geometry.

110
00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.519
This sample shows parallax UVs plugged into each text

111
00:04:55.519 --> 00:04:58.800
UV for the shift effect, but you

112
00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.639
can see the kind of pros and cons of each approach here.

113
00:05:03.639 --> 00:05:07.120
Sometimes, especially with the newer features of Unreal

114
00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.759
Engine, say, Nanite approach, you

115
00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:12.480
may just want to add the geometry in there.

116
00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.329
And especially if you're doing anything with virtual

117
00:05:15.329 --> 00:05:17.850
reality, the VR will break any kind

118
00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:22.250
of illusion of a bump offset or a parallax occlusion.

119
00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:26.040
So again, it depends on the project outcome.

120
00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.600
You want to weigh up the optimization

121
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:33.959
versus the end visual result for the user or the player

122
00:05:33.959 --> 00:05:37.180
or whoever it is that you're creating this project for.

123
00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:38.860
But hopefully, that gives you an idea

124
00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.860
about how you might use parallax occlusion mapping

125
00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.779
inside of your projects.

126
00:05:43.779 --> 00:05:46.379
Continuing with the parallax occlusion mapping,

127
00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:49.259
temporal AA can be used to jitter

128
00:05:49.259 --> 00:05:51.139
the number of rays slightly.

129
00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:54.259
It causes traces to be out of phase

130
00:05:54.259 --> 00:05:56.660
and cancels out artifacts over time.

131
00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.899
And shadows can be adjusted manually

132
00:05:59.899 --> 00:06:03.180
and might need to be for accuracy.

133
00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.420
And it must be activated in the function.

134
00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:10.040
So again, just keeping in mind this performance

135
00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.139
versus visual fidelity payoff.

136
00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.220
This approach with the shadowing can be expensive

137
00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.779
or it will be expensive, should I say.

138
00:06:18.779 --> 00:06:21.720
So the pixel depth offset can also be added

139
00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.860
for accurate intersections.

140
00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:26.220
If other geometry is introduced,

141
00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:29.459
the flatter the surface, the better in this instance.

142
00:06:29.459 --> 00:06:32.300
And then just a note here before we wrap up,

143
00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.500
if a height map is introduced,

144
00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:37.839
steep maps will have noticeable stretching.

145
00:06:37.839 --> 00:06:40.639
So to fix this, you can have a world alignment texture

146
00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:45.620
that can be used for tiling options or troubled areas.

147
00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:49.699
And then for creating your ice effect.

148
00:06:49.699 --> 00:06:53.100
For ice, you can use a world alignment material function

149
00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.220
that can be introduced to help

150
00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:56.579
with subsurface, visual matching.

151
00:06:56.579 --> 00:06:59.459
The world alignment textures help

152
00:06:59.459 --> 00:07:02.860
for textures to be consistent and matching

153
00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.959
and not based on UVs,

154
00:07:04.959 --> 00:07:07.500
but world position for the subsurface.

155
00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.540
And then with a Fresnel node and a subsurface color,

156
00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.899
they can be plugged in into that subsurface node.

157
00:07:13.899 --> 00:07:15.600
So you can see an example

158
00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.279
of exactly what we're looking at there.

159
00:07:18.279 --> 00:07:20.259
The subsurface profile,

160
00:07:20.259 --> 00:07:22.819
which you can get through right clicking

161
00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:25.019
in your content browser, going to materials

162
00:07:25.019 --> 00:07:27.220
and the subsurface profile is here.

163
00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.819
So the subsurface scouting profile data

164
00:07:29.819 --> 00:07:32.180
is an asset that can be created,

165
00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.540
shared because it's a data asset

166
00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:36.459
and saved in the content browser.

167
00:07:36.459 --> 00:07:40.899
So the data is intended to be authored by artists

168
00:07:40.899 --> 00:07:43.339
and controls the distance,

169
00:07:43.339 --> 00:07:45.899
the light and the subsurface should scatter.

170
00:07:45.899 --> 00:07:47.819
It controls the color of the subsurface

171
00:07:47.819 --> 00:07:50.139
and also the fall off color of the light

172
00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:51.939
once it has exited the object.

173
00:07:51.939 --> 00:07:56.100
So the data can then be applied to a subsurface material

174
00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.300
and then the subsurface profile data

175
00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:00.459
can be tweaked interactively,

176
00:08:00.459 --> 00:08:03.300
meaning that you do not need to recompile the material

177
00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.379
to see the end results.

178
00:08:04.379 --> 00:08:07.819
So it's just a nice global way of really dialing in

179
00:08:07.819 --> 00:08:09.139
what it is you want to do.

180
00:08:09.139 --> 00:08:11.819
And you can add it into the details panel

181
00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:14.060
of the material here with the shading model.

182
00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:16.180
You can just use a subsurface profile

183
00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.500
and then add that subsurface profile into here.

184
00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.779
So it really gives you a nice global feel

185
00:08:22.779 --> 00:08:24.620
to what it is you're looking for.

186
00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:27.339
You may have them map specific, for example,

187
00:08:27.339 --> 00:08:28.879
to really dial in that visual effects

188
00:08:28.879 --> 00:08:31.509
of the subsurface in a given level.

189
00:08:31.509 --> 00:08:34.950
So it can be created and loaded into the material

190
00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.659
and also migrated into different projects.

191
00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:42.279
If you want to keep reusing your subsurface profiles,

192
00:08:42.279 --> 00:08:43.480
has a separate menu,

193
00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.440
as you can see in the bottom right here,

194
00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.519
updates without compiling, as we said,

195
00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:51.519
and it's still not visually robust

196
00:08:51.519 --> 00:08:56.389
with the customization of apparent material.

197
00:08:56.389 --> 00:08:58.190
Few pro tips to close out then.

198
00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:00.990
So it's best to customize your subsurface material

199
00:09:01.029 --> 00:09:04.350
with full control of the properties, like you can see here.

200
00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.269
So you really want to dial in,

201
00:09:06.269 --> 00:09:09.590
you want to make sure everything's some kind of parameter

202
00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:12.730
that you can really hone in the colors

203
00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.389
and the values that you're aiming for,

204
00:09:14.389 --> 00:09:17.289
as well as any maps that you want to include.

205
00:09:17.289 --> 00:09:20.250
Mega scans export or plugin material creation.

206
00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.370
A transmission effect is not as strong

207
00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:25.169
as customizing the subsurface material.

208
00:09:25.169 --> 00:09:28.649
And you also want to create as much customization

209
00:09:28.649 --> 00:09:30.889
as you can for best control

210
00:09:30.889 --> 00:09:33.980
and results for an instant material.

211
00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:37.419
So as we usually do at the end of these sections,

212
00:09:37.419 --> 00:09:40.899
you can find subsurface profile and documentation here,

213
00:09:40.899 --> 00:09:44.620
and also just information about how to use subsurface

214
00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.500
in your Unreal Engine materials

215
00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.379
at this documentation page here.
